course: biology
lesson: 186
title: Record-breakers: the biggest and the smallest
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «Biggest» depends on how you measure: the tallest animal is the giraffe, the biggest single tree is a sequoia, but the heaviest living things may be hidden, a whole aspen forest or a fungus joined underground into one.
explain: A giant sequoia called General Sherman is so wide that a family car could park across its trunk, and it has been growing for thousands of years behind bark thick enough to survive forest fires.
explain: At the other end, a bat smaller than your thumb, a hummingbird hardly bigger than a bee and a frog that sits on a fingernail all live on the edge, because tiny bodies lose heat very fast.
explain: Sizes surprise: a lion's mane jellyfish can trail tentacles longer than a blue whale, and in Australia an earthworm over a metre long gurgles in its burrow loudly enough for people to hear.
fact: The General Sherman tree in California is the biggest single tree by volume; sequoias live for thousands of years, have thick fire-proof bark, and their cones open with fire.
fact: Pando in Utah is a group of aspen trees that are all one plant joined by the same roots, and it may be one of the heaviest living things on Earth.
fact: A honey fungus in Oregon spreads as threads through about ten square kilometres of soil, feeds on tree roots, and may be thousands of years old.
fact: The bumblebee bat, about the size of a big bumblebee and weighing about two grams, is one of the smallest mammals, and small bodies need little food but lose heat fast.
fact: The bee hummingbird from Cuba is the smallest bird in the world; it weighs about two grams and lays eggs the size of a pea.
fact: A frog from Papua New Guinea about seven millimetres long is one of the smallest animals with a backbone, living in leaf litter and eating tiny insects.
fact: The ostrich lays the biggest egg of any living bird, weighing about as much as two dozen chicken eggs, with a shell strong enough for a person to stand on.
fact: The blue whale is the biggest animal that has ever lived, but not the biggest living thing.
fact: The giraffe is the tallest animal on Earth, reaches food other animals cannot, and has seven neck bones, the same number as a person.
fact: The Goliath beetle is one of the heaviest insects; its grubs grow big on rotting wood, and a grown beetle can weigh as much as a small bird.
fact: The lion's mane jellyfish is one of the longest animals, with tentacles over thirty metres, and one found in 1870 was longer than a blue whale.
fact: The giant Gippsland earthworm of Australia can grow over a metre long, lives deep in wet soil, and people can hear it gurgling in its burrow.
exercise: Which is the biggest single tree by volume? | The General Sherman sequoia in California | An old oak in a park in the south of England | A palm in Egypt | A baobab in Madagascar
exercise: Why can giant sequoias grow so huge? | They live for thousands of years and have thick bark against fire | They grow a metre a day | They are watered by people | They grow underground first
exercise: What do sequoia cones need to open and release seeds? | Fire | Deep snow | Moonlight | Wild bees
exercise: What is Pando? | A group of aspen trees that are all one plant joined by roots | A giant panda in a zoo | A huge mushroom | A single giant sequoia tree in California, the tallest in its forest
exercise: How does the giant honey fungus in Oregon spread? | As threads through the soil, feeding on tree roots | By flying seeds | By rolling slowly down the hills | By swimming in rivers and streams to new forests far away
exercise: How big is the bumblebee bat? | About the size of a big bumblebee, around two grams | About the size of a cat | About the size of a small horse | Smaller than a grain of sand, so you need a microscope
exercise: What is the problem with having a very small body? | It loses heat fast | It needs huge meals | It cannot move about | It is too heavy to fly
exercise: Which is the smallest bird in the world? | The bee hummingbird from Cuba | The little house sparrow from Europe | The penguin from cold Antarctica | The little kiwi from New Zealand
exercise: How big are a bee hummingbird's eggs? | The size of a pea | The size of a melon | The size of a football | The size of an apple
exercise: How long is the tiny frog from Papua New Guinea? | About seven millimetres | About seven centimetres | About seven metres | About seventy centimetres
exercise: How heavy is an ostrich egg? | About as heavy as two dozen chicken eggs | About as heavy as one ordinary chicken egg from a shop | As heavy as a small family car | As light as a feather
exercise: Which is the tallest animal on Earth? | The giraffe | The elephant | The African ostrich | The giant sequoia
exercise: How many neck bones does a giraffe have? | Seven, the same as a person | Seventy, ten times as many as we do | Two hundred | One very long one
exercise: Why do Goliath beetle grubs grow so big? | They eat rotting wood and grow for a long time | They eat other beetles only, and grow fat on them | They drink milk | They live in the sea
exercise: How long can lion's mane jellyfish tentacles be? | Over thirty metres | About thirty centimetres | About three metres at most | About three kilometres
exercise: How do people know a giant Gippsland earthworm is nearby? | They can hear it gurgling in its burrow | It glows at night | It barks | It climbs trees and sits up there singing
test: «giraffe's got loads more neck bones than me, look at it» What is right? | Both have seven, the giraffe's are just much longer | Right, it has about seventy, one for every year of its life | It has only one | It has none
test: «biggest living thing = blue whale, done» What is right? | The blue whale is the biggest animal, but Pando or a giant fungus may be heavier as single living things | Right, nothing is heavier | No, the giraffe is heavier | No, the ostrich is
test: «3 ostriches, how many toes? they got 2 on each foot btw» | 12 | 6 | 18 | 30
test: «2 giraffes and an ostrich, how many legs» | 10 | 8 | 12 | 6
test: «stood on an ostrich egg, it didn't break, fake right» What is right? | Its shell is strong enough for a person to stand on | Right, it must be plastic | Ostrich eggs are made of stone and never break at all | It was a melon
test: In Kung Fu Panda a giant panda fights. Which is the smallest mammal in this lesson? | The bumblebee bat, about two grams | The giant panda, the star of the film itself | The giraffe | The beaver
test: What would happen to a bumblebee bat on a cold night without food? | It would lose heat fast and be in danger | It would grow bigger and stronger | Nothing, tiny bodies keep their heat best of all | It would sweat
test: «a bird lays eggs the size of peas? no way» What is right? | The bee hummingbird does, it is the smallest bird | No bird egg is smaller than a chicken's | Only snakes lay small eggs | It is a beetle, not a bird
test: «a whole forest that's one tree? nah» What is right? | Pando's aspens are all one plant joined by roots | Right, every tree is separate | It is a forest of plastic trees | It is one giant mushroom
test: «jellyfish longer than a blue whale, u serious» What is right? | A lion's mane found in 1870 was longer than a blue whale | No jellyfish is ever longer than about one metre, even in the deep sea | It was two jellyfish tied together | Jellyfish have no tentacles
test: What would happen to a sequoia's seeds if there were never any forest fires? | Many cones would stay closed and fewer seeds would be released | The tree would grow twice as fast | The cones would open in snow instead | Nothing, cones never open
exam: Identify the organism recognised as the largest single tree by volume. | The giant sequoia known as General Sherman, in California | An oak in England | A baobab in Madagascar | An aspen in Utah standing alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Pando? | A colony of aspen trees constituting a single plant connected by a common root system | A solitary sequoia of great age | A species of fungus confined to Oregon | A forest planted by human beings in the nineteenth century for the purposes of timber and fuel
exam: State the number of cervical vertebrae, or neck bones, possessed by the giraffe, in comparison with that of a human being. | Seven, identical in number to that of a human being | Seventy, being ten times that of a human being | Fourteen, being twice that of a human being | One, considerably elongated
exam: Ascertain the principal difficulty confronting mammals and birds of very small size. | The rapid loss of bodily heat | An excessive requirement for large meals | An inability to move | Excessive bodily weight
